53	97	50	Deal Model v1	draft	1	\N	f	3	3	2.5	4.5	0	0	0	236712	236712	710136	442598	0	156245	\N	1318992	3	439664	-322503	0	-322503	262500	131250	\N	131250	Revenue Retention	24	50000	25000	15000	2.0	1.0	5.0	fixed	fixed	percent	percent	percent	{"qoe":{"expert":"quality_of_earnings","company_name":"Palatial leisure","analysis_date":"2025-05-20","score":58,"confidence":"high","reported_figures":{"operating_profit":176360,"depreciation":59902,"amortisation":0,"reported_ebitda":236262,"source_year":2024},"add_backs":[{"item":"Director Salary Excess","category":"owner_salary","amount":16000,"recurrence":"recurring","year":2024,"justification":"Total director remuneration of £95,936 for 2 directors. Based on £5M-£10M revenue, a working MD mid-point is £77,500. Assuming one active MD and one non-working director (common in group structures), the excess is approx £16k.","confidence":"medium","defensibility_score":6,"case_for_inclusion":"The business is largely managed by group functions and a contractor workforce, suggesting director salaries are not fully reflective of market-rate operational roles.","case_for_exclusion":"The seller will argue that both directors are active and that £48k per director is already below the market benchmark for a £5m+ revenue business."}],"deductions":[{"item":"Deferred Maintenance / Capex Cliff","category":"deferred_maintenance","amount":44217,"recurrence":"recurring","year":2024,"justification":"Asset freshness is critically low at 6.6%. The 2024 depreciation charge of £59,902 is significantly lower than the 2023 charge of £208,054. A normalized maintenance budget is required to prevent facility degradation.","amount_calculation":"Estimated as the difference between 2024 depreciation and a 3-year average maintenance requirement.","confidence":"high","defensibility_score":8,"case_for_inclusion":"The sharp drop in depreciation and low asset freshness indicates the business is under-investing in its physical environment to boost short-term profit.","case_for_exclusion":"The seller may argue the disposal of freehold property reduced the overall asset base and therefore the required maintenance spend."},{"item":"Normalized Advertising Spend","category":"unsustainable_revenue","amount":143021,"recurrence":"recurring","year":2024,"justification":"Advertising spend was cut by 81% (£175k in 2023 to £32k in 2024). This level of reduction is unsustainable for maintaining a £5m+ revenue stream in the leisure sector.","confidence":"high","defensibility_score":7,"case_for_inclusion":"Current profitability is artificially inflated by a temporary cessation of marketing activities that will eventually lead to revenue decay.","case_for_exclusion":"The seller might claim the business has reached a level of maturity or brand recognition where high marketing spend is no longer required."}],"total_add_backs":16000,"total_deductions":187238,"adjusted_ebitda":{"conservative":49024,"base":65024,"optimistic":252262},"adjustment_summary":{"reported_ebitda":236262,"plus_add_backs":16000,"minus_deductions":187238,"equals_adjusted_ebitda_base":65024},"director_salary_analysis":{"directors":[{"name":"Combined Directors (2)","current_salary":95936,"estimated_market_rate":80000,"benchmark_range_used":"Revenue £5M-£10M: Working MD/Owner-Director (£65k-£90k)","adjustment":15936,"adjustment_type":"add_back"}]},"sustainability_assessment":{"sustainable_for_3_years":false,"key_assumptions":["Revenue remains stable despite the 81% cut in marketing spend.","The intercompany receivable of £5.4m is collectible and not a 'soft' asset.","The transition to a leasehold model with £389k rent is permanent."],"sensitivity_factors":["Sensitivity to occupancy/sales volume is high due to the new fixed rent cost.","Dependence on group-level support for the 2-person headcount."],"upside_scenarios":["Optimization of the £1.08m direct labor/contractor spend.","Utilization of the £442k HMRC settlement for facility upgrades."],"downside_scenarios":["Revenue decline due to lack of marketing investment.","Significant capex requirement to replace aging fixtures and fittings (Asset Freshness 6.6%)."]},"key_findings":["Reported EBITDA is stable, but underlying operating profit is extremely thin (3.28%).","The business has moved from an asset-rich freehold model to a high-overhead leasehold model.","Profitability in 2024 was significantly aided by cutting marketing and maintenance-related depreciation.","The balance sheet is now dominated by a £5.4m intercompany loan, which represents the proceeds of the property sale."],"red_flags":["Asset Freshness Ratio of 6.6% indicates an imminent 'Capex Cliff'.","81% reduction in advertising spend is a major risk to future revenue sustainability.","Operating profit is lower than the interest receivable from group companies, meaning the 'tail is wagging the dog'."],"narrative":"Palatial Leisure's 2024 performance is a 'mirage' of profitability created by aggressive cost-cutting and structural financial engineering. While Reported EBITDA is £236k, this was achieved by slashing marketing by £143k and benefiting from a lower depreciation charge following the property disposal. When these costs are normalized to sustainable levels, the Base Case Adjusted EBITDA drops to £65k. The business has traded its physical security (freehold) for a group debt of £5.4m, and the new £389k annual rent significantly increases operational gearing. A buyer should value this business on the core operating cash flow, which is substantially lower than the headline figures suggest."},"valuation":{"expert":"valuation","company_name":"Palatial leisure","analysis_date":"2025-05-20","score":65,"confidence":"medium","ebitda_multiple_valuation":{"adjusted_ebitda_base":65024,"sector_multiple_range":{"low":2.5,"mid":3.5,"high":4.5},"selected_multiple":3,"multiple_justification":"A multiple of 3.0x is selected, which is below the sector midpoint but appropriate for a business of this size (£5.3m revenue) with thin operating margins. The multiple reflects the stability of the revenue stream but is heavily tempered by the 'Capex Cliff' risk and the transition to a high-rent leasehold model which increases operational gearing.","premium_factors_applied":["Stable revenue growth (3.2%)","Negative cash conversion cycle (cash-rich operations)","Significant post-balance sheet HMRC settlement (£442k)"],"discount_factors_applied":["Critically low asset freshness (6.6%) indicating imminent CAPEX needs","Thin operating margins (3.28%)","81% reduction in advertising spend is unsustainable","High operational gearing following property disposal and leaseback"],"enterprise_value_range":{"conservative":122560,"base":195072,"optimistic":292608}},"ev_to_equity_bridge":{"enterprise_value_base":195072,"plus_cash":442598,"minus_long_term_debt":0,"minus_director_loans":156245,"minus_bad_debt_provision":0,"minus_working_capital_adjustment":0,"equity_value":{"conservative":408913,"base":481425,"optimistic":578961}},"asset_based_valuation":{"net_asset_value":8919704,"property_adjustment":0,"bad_debt_adjustment":0,"adjusted_nav":8919704,"nav_as_floor":true},"revenue_multiple_crosscheck":{"latest_revenue":5383416,"revenue_multiple_range":{"low":0.4,"high":0.8},"implied_ev_range":{"low":2153366,"high":4306732}},"valuation_summary":{"recommended_range":{"equity_value_low":450000,"equity_value_mid":500000,"equity_value_high":600000},"primary_methodology":"ebitda_multiple","cross_check_alignment":"weak"},"sensitivity":{"ebitda_plus_10pct":{"enterprise_value":214579,"equity_value":500932},"ebitda_minus_10pct":{"enterprise_value":175565,"equity_value":461918},"multiple_plus_half":{"enterprise_value":227584,"equity_value":513937},"multiple_minus_half":{"enterprise_value":162560,"equity_value":448913},"key_customer_loss_impact":"Low impact due to diversified B2C customer base.","director_replacement_impact":16000},"seller_expectations":{"likely_seller_expectation_range":{"low":2500000,"high":6000000},"seller_anchor_points":["Reported Net Profit of £483k","Intercompany receivable of £5.4m","Historical property value prior to disposal","Revenue multiple of 1x (£5.3m)"],"negotiation_gap_assessment":"Extreme. The seller will likely anchor on the £5.4m intercompany receivable and the headline net profit. However, a buyer will focus on the adjusted EBITDA of £65k. The gap is driven by the seller viewing the intercompany debt as 'cash' and the buyer viewing it as a 'soft' asset of a complex group."},"key_findings":["Reported EBITDA of £236k is unsustainable due to aggressive cuts in marketing and maintenance.","Adjusted EBITDA of £65k reflects the true operational earnings power.","The business has traded tangible freehold security for a £5.4m intercompany loan.","The impending £442k HMRC settlement is the primary driver of short-term equity value.","Asset freshness of 6.6% suggests a significant capital expenditure requirement is imminent."],"red_flags":["Operating margin is dangerously thin at 3.28%.","81% reduction in advertising spend creates a high risk of future revenue decay.","The balance sheet is dominated by intercompany debt, making the entity's solvency dependent on the wider group.","Discrepancy between reported headcount (2) and direct labor costs (£1.08m) suggests hidden operational complexity."],"narrative":"Valuing Palatial Leisure requires a strict distinction between 'accounting profit' and 'maintainable cash flow'. While the 2024 accounts show a surge in net profit, this is a result of non-operating interest income and unsustainable cost-cutting (marketing and depreciation). On an adjusted basis, the business generates a modest £65k EBITDA. The primary value for an acquirer lies in the stable £5.3m revenue base and the £442k HMRC cash injection. However, the transition to a leasehold model has increased fixed costs, and the aging fixtures (6.6% freshness) necessitate a price discount to fund future CAPEX. The massive £5.4m intercompany receivable creates a significant valuation bridge issue; unless this is settled in cash at completion, the equity value remains modest. A deal is only likely if the seller accepts a valuation based on core operations rather than the restructured balance sheet."},"deal_structure":{"expert":"deal_structure","company_name":"Palatial leisure","analysis_date":"2025-05-20","score":55,"confidence":"high","structures":[{"name":"Conservative Approach","strategy":"Heavy emphasis on performance verification and protection against the 'Capex Cliff' and unsustainable cost-cutting.","risk_to_buyer":"low","suitable_when":"The buyer is concerned that the 81% reduction in marketing will lead to a revenue collapse and that the aging assets require immediate replacement.","consideration":{"total_headline":500000,"upfront_cash":150000,"upfront_percent":30,"deferred":150000,"earnout_maximum":200000,"earnout_metric":"Adjusted EBITDA","earnout_period_months":36,"earnout_rationale":"Trigger 4 (Unverified add-backs/uncertain earnings): The QoE expert identified that reported EBITDA is inflated by unsustainable cuts in marketing (£143k) and maintenance. The earnout ensures the buyer only pays for the 'mirage' if it proves to be maintainable over 3 years.","retention_escrow":100000},"key_conditions":["Settlement of £5.4m intercompany receivable in cash prior to completion or formal exclusion from equity value.","HMRC settlement of £442,598 to be paid directly into a buyer-controlled escrow.","Minimum 12-month handover from both directors."],"notes":"Seller must provide a 12-month handover and a 36-month non-compete within a 50-mile radius. Key warranties include a specific indemnity for the HMRC settlement and a warranty on the operational state of fixtures/fittings. Warranty cap at 50% of consideration with a 24-month period. Completion accounts mechanism with a working capital peg excluding intercompany balances. Risk is low for the buyer due to the high earnout and escrow protection."},{"name":"Balanced Approach","strategy":"A fair split that acknowledges the stable revenue history while protecting against the immediate need for facility reinvestment.","risk_to_buyer":"medium","suitable_when":"The seller is willing to accept a valuation based on adjusted earnings rather than the £5.4m intercompany debt anchor.","consideration":{"total_headline":525000,"upfront_cash":262500,"upfront_percent":50,"deferred":131250,"earnout_maximum":131250,"earnout_metric":"Revenue Retention","earnout_period_months":24,"earnout_rationale":"Trigger 2 (Revenue stagnation risk): Due to the 81% cut in advertising, there is a high risk of revenue decay. This earnout protects the buyer if the £5.3m revenue base falls below a £5m floor post-acquisition.","retention_escrow":50000},"key_conditions":["Formalization of the lease agreement for the property at market rates.","Evidence of contractor/agency labor contracts to mitigate the 2-person headcount risk.","Director loan accounts (£156k) to be cleared at completion."],"notes":"6-month handover period followed by 12 months of consultancy. Standard warranty package with a 100% cap on upfront cash. Locked-box mechanism based on the 2024 accounts, provided no leakage occurs via intercompany transfers. Risk is medium as the buyer takes on more upfront cost while the 'Capex Cliff' remains a looming operational requirement."},{"name":"Competitive Approach","strategy":"Maximizing upfront cash to bridge the gap with seller expectations, relying on strong legal indemnities rather than contingent payments.","risk_to_buyer":"high","suitable_when":"A competitive bidding environment where the buyer values the £5.3m revenue stream and the negative cash conversion cycle highly.","consideration":{"total_headline":600000,"upfront_cash":450000,"upfront_percent":75,"deferred":150000,"earnout_maximum":0,"earnout_metric":"N/A","earnout_period_months":0,"earnout_rationale":"No earnout included to increase offer competitiveness; risk is instead managed through a lower headline price relative to seller expectations and strict completion adjustments.","retention_escrow":442000},"key_conditions":["Full retention of the HMRC settlement amount (£442k) until cash is received by the company.","Personal guarantees from directors regarding the recoverability of any remaining intercompany balances.","Immediate 25% increase in marketing budget post-completion."],"notes":"3-month handover period. Lighter warranty requirements but with a specific 'Capex Indemnity' if major equipment fails within 6 months. Completion accounts mechanism. Risk is high because the buyer is paying a premium (high end of valuation) for a business with thin margins and significant deferred maintenance."}],"recommended_structure":"Conservative Approach","recommendation_rationale":"The 'Conservative Approach' is recommended due to the extreme discrepancy between reported and adjusted EBITDA, the 'Capex Cliff' (6.6% asset freshness), and the massive valuation gap with the seller. The business is currently a 'financial mirage' sustained by cutting essential marketing and maintenance. A buyer must protect themselves against the likely scenario that margins will compress further once sustainable spending levels are restored.","negotiation_priorities":["De-linking the £5.4m intercompany receivable from the operating company's valuation.","Securing the £442k HMRC settlement as a buyer-side benefit or price reduction.","Establishing a realistic 'Maintenance Capex' budget as a deduction from the purchase price.","Ensuring the 2-person headcount is supplemented by a transition plan for the contractor workforce."],"deal_breaker_terms":["Refusal to settle or exclude the £5.4m intercompany debt from the equity bridge.","Inability to secure a long-term, market-rate lease on the operational property.","Discovery that the £1.08m 'direct labor' cost hides significant undisclosed liabilities or legal risks."],"key_findings":["The business has high operational gearing due to the new £389k rent expense.","Reported profits are heavily reliant on non-operating interest income from group loans.","The 81% cut in advertising is a 'ticking time bomb' for revenue sustainability.","Asset freshness of 6.6% is among the lowest seen in the sector, indicating imminent cash outflows for equipment."],"narrative":"Structuring a deal for Palatial Leisure is a challenge of 'valuation vs. reality.' The seller is anchored to a multi-million pound figure based on intercompany debt and headline profits. However, the operational reality is a thin-margin business (3.28% operating margin) that has been 'hollowed out' by cutting marketing and maintenance. The recommended structure uses a heavy earnout to force the seller to prove that the current revenue is sustainable without the historical marketing spend. Furthermore, the deal must be contingent on the HMRC settlement and the rationalization of the complex intercompany structure, which currently makes the entity's solvency dependent on the wider group."},"financial":{"expert":"financial","company_name":"Palatial leisure","analysis_date":"2025-05-20","score":68,"confidence":"high","yearly_metrics":{"2023":{"revenue":5215031,"revenue_yoy_growth_percent":null,"cost_of_sales":3070866,"gross_profit":2144165,"gross_margin_percent":41.12,"administrative_expenses":2185871,"operating_profit":29108,"operating_margin_percent":0.56,"depreciation_amortisation":208054,"ebitda":237162,"interest_payable":19543,"interest_coverage_ratio":1.49,"profit_before_tax":206564,"corporation_tax":74441,"effective_tax_rate_percent":36.04,"net_profit":132123,"net_margin_percent":2.53},"2024":{"revenue":5383416,"revenue_yoy_growth_percent":3.23,"cost_of_sales":3127907,"gross_profit":2255509,"gross_margin_percent":41.9,"administrative_expenses":2148056,"operating_profit":176360,"operating_margin_percent":3.28,"depreciation_amortisation":59902,"ebitda":236262,"interest_payable":2216,"interest_coverage_ratio":79.58,"profit_before_tax":424441,"corporation_tax":-58823,"effective_tax_rate_percent":-13.86,"net_profit":483264,"net_margin_percent":8.98}},"cagr":{"revenue_cagr_percent":3.23,"gross_profit_cagr_percent":5.19,"operating_profit_cagr_percent":505.88,"period_years":1},"cost_structure":{"cost_of_sales_percent_revenue":58.1,"overheads_percent_revenue":39.9,"staff_costs_percent_revenue":22.3,"director_costs_percent_revenue":1.78,"top_5_overheads":[{"category":"Other Overheads","value":657433,"percent_of_overheads":30.6},{"category":"Staff Wages & Salaries","value":1079758,"percent_of_overheads":50.27},{"category":"Utilities","value":472189,"percent_of_overheads":21.98},{"category":"Rent & Rates","value":389170,"percent_of_overheads":18.12},{"category":"Repairs & Maintenance","value":104119,"percent_of_overheads":4.85}],"cost_base_classification":"mixed"},"earnings_quality":{"net_profit_tracks_operating_profit":false,"exceptional_items_identified":["HMRC settlement of £442,598 received post-year end","Significant tax credit in 2024","High interest receivable (£250k) inflating PBT"],"tax_rate_consistent":false,"ebitda_latest":236262,"ebitda_trend":"stable","limitations":["Depreciation significantly reduced in 2024 due to property disposal","Operating profit is low relative to non-operating income"]},"debt_service":{"latest_interest_coverage":79.58,"interest_coverage_trend":"improving","additional_debt_capacity_assessment":"High capacity based on current interest coverage, but core operating cash flow is thin."},"key_findings":["Revenue growth is modest at 3.2%, suggesting a mature or stable market position.","Operating margins improved from 0.56% to 3.28%, largely due to reduced advertising and depreciation.","Net profit is heavily distorted by non-operating items, including £250k in interest receivable and a tax credit.","The company underwent a major structural change in 2024, disposing of £5.77m in freehold property.","Balance sheet liquidity is high but dominated by intercompany receivables (£5.41m) rather than external cash.","A post-balance sheet HMRC settlement of £442k provides a significant one-off cash injection for 2025.","Core EBITDA has remained remarkably flat at approx £236k-£237k despite the jump in net profit."],"red_flags":["Core operating profit is weak, representing only 3.28% of revenue.","Heavy reliance on interest income from group undertakings to achieve bottom-line profitability.","Significant reduction in advertising spend (81%) may impact future revenue growth.","Asset base has shifted from tangible property to intercompany debt, increasing counterparty risk."],"narrative":"Palatial Leisure presents a complex financial profile characterized by stable top-line performance but low core operational profitability. While the 2024 net profit shows a dramatic 265% increase, a senior analyst must look beyond this headline figure. The improvement is primarily driven by non-operating factors: a substantial increase in interest receivable from group companies, a significant tax credit, and a sharp reduction in depreciation following the disposal of its freehold property portfolio. \\n\\nFrom an operational standpoint, the business is lean but faces thin margins. The gross margin is stable at 41.9%, but the operating margin of 3.28% leaves little room for error. The decision to cut advertising spend by over 80% in 2024 helped boost the current year's profit but raises concerns regarding the sustainability of revenue growth in future periods. \\n\\nThe balance sheet has been fundamentally restructured. By transferring its freehold assets to a connected entity and replacing them with a £5.41m intercompany receivable, the company has traded tangible security for group debt. While the current ratio appears exceptionally strong at 9.36, the quality of this liquidity is entirely dependent on the debt-servicing capacity of the wider group. \\n\\nFor an acquirer, the primary value lies in the stable revenue stream and the impending £442k HMRC settlement. However, the 'true' earnings power (EBITDA) has remained static at approximately £236k. The acquisition should be priced on this core operating cash flow rather than the inflated net profit figure. The lack of external debt provides significant capacity for acquisition leverage, provided the core operations can be optimized to improve the underlying operating margin."}}	{"balanceSheet":{"id":362,"financialPeriodId":440,"targetCompanyId":97,"intangibleAssets":null,"tangibleFixedAssets":"391763","freeholdProperty":"0","leaseholdProperty":null,"plantMachinery":null,"fixturesFittings":"391763","motorVehicles":"0","computerEquipment":null,"stockInventory":"95755","tradeDebtors":"5743","prepayments":"83605","amountsOwedByDirectors":"156245","amountsOwedByConnectedCompanies":"5414051","directorsCurrentAccount":"156245","otherDebtors":"282118","cashAtBank":"1030422","totalCurrentAssets":"9986248","tradeCreditors":"380765","taxationSocialSecurity":"242008","vatPayable":null,"corporationTaxDue":null,"bankLoansOverdraftCurrent":"0","hirePurchaseCurrent":null,"accruals":"122128","amountsOwedToDirectors":"231825","otherCreditors":"89818","totalCurrentLiabilities":"1","bankLoansLongTerm":null,"hirePurchaseLongTerm":null,"deferredTaxation":"0","otherLongTermDebt":null,"totalLongTermLiabilities":"0","shareCapital":"102","retainedEarnings":"9311367","totalEquity":"9311469","debtorsList":null,"creditorsList":null,"fixedAssetsDetail":null,"sourceDocumentId":506,"createdAt":"2026-04-25T14:37:03.542Z","updatedAt":"2026-04-25T14:38:22.347Z"},"fixedAssets":null}	\N	2026-04-25 15:03:41.198933	2026-04-25 15:32:34.318	share_sale
