xpected_case":1250000,"worst_case":1050000}}],"recommended_structure":"Balanced","recommendation_rationale":"The Balanced structure addresses the primary tension: the seller's high valuation expectations versus the buyer's need to fund a 'capex cliff' and fix payroll inefficiencies. By providing 75% upfront, the buyer remains competitive, while the earnout and specific indemnities for racking/ERP protect against the most immediate operational risks identified in the assessments.","negotiation_priorities":["Normalization of the £147.5k distribution cost as a one-off.","Agreement on the £518k working capital peg to protect against creditor stretching.","Securing the deferred maintenance indemnity for the warehouse racking.","Defining the 'Adjusted EBITDA' metric for the earnout to exclude buyer-side integration costs."],"deal_breaker_terms":["Reduction in supplier credit terms below 60 days prior to completion.","Discovery of significant stock obsolescence during the pre-completion count.","Resignation of both key management personnel before the handover period is agreed."],"key_findings":["The business is a 'cash cow' but requires immediate modernization of IT and warehouse systems.","Payroll costs are the primary area for value creation (reversing the 21% revenue ratio).","The clean balance sheet (no director loans) makes it highly 'bankable' for acquisition finance.","The 94-day creditor cycle is a hidden risk that must be managed via the working capital peg."],"narrative":"Test Company is an ideal 'platform' acquisition. It has the stability of 29% gross margins and a diversified customer base, but it has become operationally 'lazy' with payroll costs doubling. The deal structure must bridge the gap between the seller's view of a £3.2M revenue business and the buyer's view of an aging infrastructure. A Balanced structure with a clear EBITDA-based earnout incentivizes the seller to assist in the productivity transition while ensuring the buyer doesn't overpay for 'exceptional' costs that might actually be recurring."},"financial":{"expert":"financial","company_name":"Test Company","analysis_date":"2024-05-22","score":82,"confidence":"high","yearly_metrics":{"2020":{"revenue":2845000,"revenue_yoy_growth_percent":null,"cost_of_sales":2019750,"gross_profit":825250,"gross_margin_percent":29.01,"administrative_expenses":498600,"operating_profit":184400,"operating_margin_percent":6.48,"depreciation_amortisation":35100,"ebitda":219500,"interest_payable":8200,"interest_coverage_ratio":22.49,"profit_before_tax":176200,"corporation_tax":33500,"effective_tax_rate_percent":19.01,"net_profit":142700,"net_margin_percent":5.02},"2021":{"revenue":2912000,"revenue_yoy_growth_percent":2.36,"cost_of_sales":2064320,"gross_profit":847680,"gross_margin_percent":29.11,"administrative_expenses":515300,"operating_profit":186380,"operating_margin_percent":6.4,"depreciation_amortisation":36800,"ebitda":223180,"interest_payable":7500,"interest_coverage_ratio":24.85,"profit_before_tax":178880,"corporation_tax":34000,"effective_tax_rate_percent":19.01,"net_profit":144880,"net_margin_percent":4.98},"2022":{"revenue":3048000,"revenue_yoy_growth_percent":4.67,"cost_of_sales":2158080,"gross_profit":889920,"gross_margin_percent":29.2,"administrative_expenses":537800,"operating_profit":199720,"operating_margin_percent":6.55,"depreciation_amortisation":38200,"ebitda":237920,"interest_payable":6800,"interest_coverage_ratio":29.37,"profit_before_tax":192920,"corporation_tax":36700,"effective_tax_rate_percent":19.02,"net_profit":156220,"net_margin_percent":5.13},"2023":{"revenue":3135000,"revenue_yoy_growth_percent":2.85,"cost_of_sales":2219850,"gross_profit":915150,"gross_margin_percent":29.19,"administrative_expenses":556200,"operating_profit":202200,"operating_margin_percent":6.45,"depreciation_amortisation":39500,"ebitda":241700,"interest_payable":5900,"interest_coverage_ratio":34.27,"profit_before_tax":196300,"corporation_tax":37300,"effective_tax_rate_percent