x","working_capital_peg":null,"rationale":"Speeds up the closing process; requires a strict 'no leakage' clause to prevent further dividends or loans before completion."},"effective_price":{"best_case":175000,"expected_case":157500,"worst_case":105000}}],"recommended_structure":"Conservative","recommendation_rationale":"The business is currently in a liquidity crisis (Quick Ratio 0.09) and is being used as a personal treasury. The 1% net margin provides no safety buffer. A conservative structure is the only way to ensure the buyer doesn't inherit an insolvent shell. The intercompany loan must be treated as a deal-breaker or a 100% retention item.","negotiation_priorities":["Recovery or offset of the £185,000 intercompany loan","Verification of subcontractor contract stability","Normalization of management costs (replacement salary)","Cessation of dividend payments prior to completion"],"deal_breaker_terms":["Refusal to guarantee or repay the intercompany loan","Further deterioration of the gross margin below 12%","Evidence that subcontractors are personally tied to the director via side-agreements"],"key_findings":["The business has a massive valuation gap: Seller likely wants £600k+ (revenue-based) while earnings support only £110k.","Liquidity has been intentionally drained, leaving the company unable to pay creditors from cash.","The 1% net margin makes the business highly fragile to even minor cost increases.","Owner dependency is 100%; the business is a 'shell' for the director's activities."],"narrative":"Goldwire Financial Solutions is a high-growth but low-value entity. The impressive 45% CAGR is hollowed out by a 1% net margin and a balance sheet where 92% of current assets are tied up in an unproven intercompany loan. Structuring a deal requires a 'back-to-basics' approach: the buyer must ignore the revenue multiples and focus on cash recovery and margin protection. The Conservative structure is recommended because it forces the seller to stand behind the 