n 2025 (£21,440 vs £1,800 in 2024)","Significant intercompany loan movement (£185,000)","Volatile pension contributions used to manage taxable profit"],"tax_rate_consistent":false,"ebitda_latest":26523,"ebitda_trend":"stable","limitations":["Amortisation not separately disclosed from depreciation in all years","Subcontractor-heavy model suggests potential off-balance sheet risks"]},"debt_service":{"latest_interest_coverage":7.85,"interest_coverage_trend":"improving","additional_debt_capacity_assessment":"Limited. While coverage is improving, absolute EBITDA is low (£26.5k), leaving little room for significant acquisition debt service."},"key_findings":["Revenue has grown at a 45% CAGR, but this has not translated into bottom-line value, with operating profit actually declining over the same period.","Gross margins have collapsed from 33.4% in 2020 to 13.8% in 2025, indicating a loss of pricing power or significant inflation in subcontractor costs.","The business model is highly reliant on subcontractors, which accounts for nearly 100% of the Cost of Sales, making the business a low-margin pass-through entity.","Net profit margins are dangerously thin (approx. 1%), making the company highly vulnerable to even minor cost increases or revenue dips.","A significant red flag exists in the 2025 balance sheet where £185,000 (nearly all cash) was moved into intercompany loans, collapsing the quick ratio to 0.09.","Dividend policy is aggressive; the £25,000 annual payout consistently exceeds net profit, leading to a steady erosion of the company's equity base.","Administrative expenses are heavily influenced by discretionary items like directors' pensions and professional fees, suggesting earnings are being managed for tax purposes."],"red_flags":["Severe gross margin compression (58% decline in margin percentage over 5 years).","Quick ratio collapse from 1.46 to 0.09 in a single year due to intercompany lending.","Dividends consistently paid out of capital rather than earnings.","High gearing relative to the thin earnings base and declining net assets."],"narrative":"Goldwire Financial Solutions Limited presents a profile of high-volume, low-margin growth that has failed to achieve operational scale. While the 45% revenue CAGR is superficially impressive, the underlying financial health is concerning. The most critical issue is the systematic compression of the gross margin, which has fallen from 33.4% to 13.8%. This suggests that as the company scales, it is losing control over its direct costs—primarily subcontractors—or is forced to price aggressively to win business in a commoditised market.\\n\\nProfitability is marginal at best. With a net margin of just 1.04%, the business has no safety buffer. Furthermore, the quality of the balance sheet has deteriorated significantly in the latest period. The decision to move £185,000 into intercompany loans has stripped the business of its liquidity, leaving it with a quick ratio of 0.09. This, combined with a dividend policy that regularly exceeds annual net profit, indicates a strategy of capital extraction rather than reinvestment. For an acquirer, the business offers a significant customer base but requires a complete overhaul of the cost structure and a stabilisation of margins. The current earnings are too thin to support any meaningful acquisition debt, and the reliance on intercompany receivables for working capital poses a significant integration risk."}}	{"balanceSheet":{"id":79,"financialPeriodId":94,"targetCompanyId":18,"intangibleAssets":null,"tangibleFixedAssets":"14827","freeholdProperty":null,"leaseholdProperty":null,"plantMachinery":null,"fixturesFittings":"0","motorVehicles":"14827","computerEquipment":"0","stockInventory":null,"tradeDebtors":"1400","prepayments":null,"amountsOwedByDirectors":null,"amountsOwedByConnectedCompanies":"185000","directorsCurrentAccount":null,"otherDebtors":"750","cashAtBank":"13239","totalCurrentAssets":"200389","tradeCreditors":"62241","taxati