y requirements but with a specific indemnity for any customer loss >10% of revenue prior to completion. Completion accounts mechanism. Risk is high as the buyer is paying 2x the earnings-based value, relying entirely on the asset floor and synergy potential."}],"recommended_structure":"Conservative Approach","recommendation_rationale":"The 'Extreme' negotiation gap and the 4-year terminal decline make any offer based on assets highly risky. The business is a 'melting ice cube' (QoE Expert) and the high customer concentration (62% in top 5) creates a cliff-edge risk. The Conservative Approach protects the buyer by only paying for the earnings value and making 40% of that contingent on the seller proving the business has stabilized.","negotiation_priorities":["Bridge the £6.2m gap between Earnings Value and Net Asset Value","Secure the top 5 customers who hold 62% of the company's value","Halt the aggressive dividend extraction identified in the Risk Assessment","Validate the management layer's ability to operate without the current owners"],"deal_breaker_terms":["Loss of 'Business Supplies UK' or any two of the top 5 customers","Further revenue decline exceeding 15% in the 2024 management accounts","Refusal of a Net Working Capital peg that protects the £2.5m cash balance"],"key_findings":["Massive disconnect between £11.4m asset value and £5.2m earnings value.","Terminal revenue decline of 10% CAGR over 4 years is the primary deal risk.","High customer concentration makes the business vulnerable to single-point failure.","The business is being 'harvested' by owners, with dividends depleting net assets."],"narrative":"Latest company Ltd is a high-quality operational shell that is losing its market relevance. While the balance sheet is strong, the P&L is in a state of controlled collapse. A successful deal must ignore the seller's anchor point of Net Asset Value and focus on the sustainable cash flow, which is significantly lower. The structure must use the top 5 customers as a 'security' for the deferred payments. If the seller insists on the £11.4m NAV, the deal should only proceed if the buyer has an immediate use for the Sheffield facility that does not depend on the existing, shrinking revenue stream."}	\N	45	high	16717	{"promptTokenCount":7024,"candidatesTokenCount":1850,"totalTokenCount":10082}	34	{valuation,risk,operational,qoe}	2026-04-21 10:16:06.701583	2026-04-21 10:17:07.547
