on.","Obtain a specific indemnity for the imminent IT/ERP replacement costs.","Verify the sustainability of the 40% gross margin under new management."],"deal_breaker_terms":["Reduction in supplier credit terms below 90 days prior to completion.","Discovery of further revenue decline (>15%) in the current trailing 12 months.","Refusal of the seller to accept a revenue-based earnout."],"key_findings":["The business is a 'cash cow' being aggressively milked, evidenced by declining net assets despite high profits.","The 121-day creditor cycle is a major hidden liquidity risk.","The £10.6m asset base provides a valuation floor but does not protect the P&L from margin erosion if revenue continues to fall.","High average salaries (£69k) suggest an opportunity for cost rationalization but also a risk of key person dependency."],"narrative":"Acme Manufacturing Ltd presents a classic 'value trap' scenario. On paper, the 40% margins and £3.16m adjusted EBITDA are attractive, but the 10% annual revenue decline is structural and persistent. The seller is likely extracting capital to the detriment of the business's future (low CAPEX, declining net assets). The recommended Conservative structure treats the acquisition as an asset-backed turnaround. By paying 50% upfront—roughly equivalent to the Net Asset Value—the buyer protects their downside, while the earnout ensures the seller only receives a premium if they can prove the business has stabilized. This structure directly addresses the 'Revenue Contraction' and 'Capital Extraction' risks identified by the experts."}	\N	55	high	13104	{"promptTokenCount":6880,"candidatesTokenCount":2461,"totalTokenCount":9341}	1	{valuation,risk,operational,qoe}	2026-04-16 18:31:03.904434	2026-04-16 18:32:05.435
